---
source: crates/sutura-app/tests/golden.rs
expression: pinned.definitions()
---
models:
  customers:
    name: customers
    source: local
    table: customers
    columns:
      - id
      - region_code
      - segment
    description: "One row per customer. Attributes only: nothing here is additive, so joining to it\ncannot change a measure."
  orders:
    name: orders
    source: local
    table: orders
    columns:
      - amount_cents
      - channel
      - customer_id
      - order_date
      - order_id
    description: "One row per order, as booked.\n\nMoney is held in minor units so that a total is exact. A metric that summed a\ndecimal column would depend on how two languages happen to print the same bits,\nand an anchor comparison over that is a test that fails for the wrong reason."
relationships:
  orders_customer:
    name: orders_customer
    origin_model: orders
    origin_column: customer_id
    target_model: customers
    target_column: id
    join_type: many_to_one
metrics:
  average_order:
    name: average_order
    model: orders
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: avg
        column: amount_cents
    required_filters: []
    time_column: order_date
    grains:
      - month
    dimensions: {}
    anchor: ~
    description: "Mean booked order value, in minor units.\n\nDeliberately carries no anchor. An average is not exact in binary, so pinning one\nas text would be pinning a formatting decision rather than a number. The two\nmetrics that do carry anchors are integer sums, where the comparison is exact.\n\nDeclared beside `average_order_value`, which is the same question asked as a\nratio: a sum over a distinct count of the order key. Both are kept, because the\nmean of a column and the mean per order are different definitions that happen to\nagree over this catalog."
  average_order_value:
    name: average_order_value
    model: orders
    measure:
      ratio:
        numerator:
          aggregate: sum
          column: amount_cents
        denominator:
          aggregate: count_distinct
          column: order_id
        zero_denominator: yields_null
    required_filters: []
    time_column: order_date
    grains:
      - day
      - month
    dimensions: {}
    anchor: ~
    description: "Booked value per order, in minor units.\n\nThe same figure as `average_order` over this catalog, and not the same\ndefinition. `avg(amount_cents)` is the mean of a column: it averages whatever\nrows the statement produced. This one averages orders, by dividing a sum by a\ndistinct count of the order key, and stays per-order regardless of what the rows\ndid on the way. They agree here because `orders` has one row per order and the\nonly declared relationship is many-to-one, which cannot fan a row out. That\nagreement is a property of this catalog rather than of the two definitions, and\nonly one of them keeps saying \"per order\" if the shape of the statement changes.\n\n`zero_denominator` is written out because a period with no orders has to mean\nsomething, and both answers are defensible. Here it means null: a day with no\norders is a day with no average, which is a different statement from a day whose\naverage is zero.\n\nCarries no anchor, for the reason `average_order` does not: a division is not\nexact in binary, so pinning one as text would pin a formatting decision rather\nthan a number."
  orders_placed:
    name: orders_placed
    model: orders
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: count
        column: order_id
    required_filters: []
    time_column: order_date
    grains:
      - day
      - month
    dimensions:
      region:
        name: region
        column: region_code
        via: orders_customer
        allowed_values:
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: ""
    anchor:
      range:
        start:
          year: 2026
          month: 6
          day: 1
        end:
          year: 2026
          month: 7
          day: 1
      value: "9"
    description: "How many orders were booked.\n\nCounts the order key rather than rows. Over a joined result those are different\nnumbers, and the one that means \"orders\" is the key."
  revenue:
    name: revenue
    model: orders
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: sum
        column: amount_cents
    required_filters: []
    time_column: order_date
    grains:
      - day
      - month
    dimensions:
      channel:
        name: channel
        column: channel
        via: ~
        allowed_values:
          - store
          - web
        description: "Where the order was placed. On the order itself, so no join."
      region:
        name: region
        column: region_code
        via: orders_customer
        allowed_values:
          - north
          - south
          - west
        description: "The customer region, reached through one declared relationship."
      segment:
        name: segment
        column: segment
        via: orders_customer
        allowed_values: ~
        description: "Declared without a value list, so it can be grouped by and not filtered on.\n"
    anchor:
      range:
        start:
          year: 2026
          month: 6
          day: 1
        end:
          year: 2026
          month: 7
          day: 1
      value: "570022"
    description: "Total booked order value, in minor units.\n\nThe anchor is the number this metric produced for June 2026 when it was\ncertified. It is re-executed on every run and at startup: a definition that has\nstopped meaning what it claimed fails readiness rather than answering."
  web_revenue:
    name: web_revenue
    model: orders
    measure:
      simple:
        aggregate: sum
        column: amount_cents
    required_filters:
      - equals:
          column: channel
          value: web
    time_column: order_date
    grains:
      - day
      - month
    dimensions: {}
    anchor: ~
    description: "Booked order value from the web channel, in minor units.\n\nThe filter is part of the name. `web_revenue` does not mean \"revenue, which you\nmay narrow to web if you remember to\": it means the web number, and a statement\nthat left the predicate out would return total revenue under this metric's\ncertified name. That is the failure this repository exists to prevent, arrived\nat by omission rather than by tampering, so the predicate is applied to every\nquestion about the metric and a caller can neither see it nor turn it off.\n\nIt is also why `channel` is not declared as a dimension here. A dimension is\nsomething a caller may group by or filter on, and either would be a way to ask\n`web_revenue` for the store figure. The metric that answers \"how does revenue\nsplit by channel\" is `revenue`, which declares the dimension and carries no\nrequired filter."
